The extended attribute calls without the Linux option (ADR 0256): their
sysdep tags and signatures leave the Linux option's block, so a system
layer without that option can implement them, and <abi-bits/xattr.h>
no longer refuses to be included without it. The Radix system layer
builds the Linux option's setxattr, getxattr, listxattr, removexattr and
their l and f forms (options/linux/generic/sys-xattr.cpp) and installs
its <sys/xattr.h> (ports/mlibc/build.sh), which need nothing of Linux's;
the BSDs have calls of their own for the same attributes.

diff --git a/abis/linux/xattr.h b/abis/linux/xattr.h
--- a/abis/linux/xattr.h
+++ b/abis/linux/xattr.h
@@ -3,10 +3,6 @@
 
 #include <mlibc-config.h>
 
-#if !__MLIBC_LINUX_OPTION
-#  error "<sys/xattr.h> is inherently Linux specific. Enable the Linux option or do not use this header."
-#endif /* !__MLIBC_LINUX_OPTION */
-
 /* __USE_KERNEL_XATTR_DEFS is exported when XATTR_* are emitted, and
  * __UAPI_DEF_XATTR is used to determine the behaviour of the
  * <linux/xattr.h> header (through <linux/libc-compat.h>), if it's set
